[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Vue 3 Composition API vs Options API: A Technical Comparison"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When developing applications in Vue.js 3, developers are presented with two distinct ways to write component logic: the traditional Options API and the newer Composition API. Understanding the differences, architectural advantages, and ideal use cases for each will help you architect scalable and maintainable Vue applications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Options API"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Options API is the classic way of writing Vue components, available in Vue 2 and fully supported in Vue 3. It organizes component logic using objects containing predefined properties such as data, methods, computed, and mounted. This structure provides a clear convention that beginners find easy to follow."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"<script>
export default {
  data() {
    return {
      count: 0
    };
  },
  methods: {
    increment() {
      this.count++;
    }
  },
  computed: {
    doubleCount() {
      return this.count * 2;
    }
  }
};
</script>"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"The Composition API"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Introduced in Vue 3, the Composition API provides a set of additive, function-based APIs that allow flexible composition of component logic. Instead of forcing code into specific options blocks, it leverages JavaScript functions (such as ref and computed) inside a setup function or the <script setup> syntactic sugar."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"<script setup>
import { ref, computed } from 'vue';

const count = ref(0);
const increment = () => count.value++;
const doubleCount = computed(() => count.value * 2);
</script>"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences and Advantages"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Logic Reusability: The Composition API enables superior logic extraction and reuse through custom composables, avoiding the mixin naming collision issues of the Options API."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Code Organization: In large Options API components, related features are scattered across data, methods, and computed properties. The Composition API groups related code together, improving readability."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"TypeScript Support: The Composition API offers much better inference and type safety with TypeScript, as it relies heavily on plain variables and functions."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion and Best Practices"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"While the Options API remains fully supported and is suitable for small, straightforward components, the Composition API is the recommended standard for large-scale enterprise applications in Vue 3 due to its scalability and modern TypeScript integration."}]
